/*
 * qnx_can_adapter.c — TEMPLATE for future real QNX CAN driver integration.
 *
 * This file is NOT compiled by default. It is a stub showing where the
 * real hardware adapter would be inserted tomorrow when the Raspberry Pi
 * BSP and CAN hardware are available.
 *
 * IMPORTANT: Do NOT invent QNX CAN APIs without verifying the BSP.
 *
 * On QNX 8, CAN support is BSP/driver dependent. Typical approaches:
 *
 *  Situation A — QNX BSP provides a CAN driver (e.g., MCP2515 via SPI,
 *                 or SJA1000, or vendor-specific):
 *    - Driver exposes a character device, e.g., /dev/can1 or /dev/can_tx0
 *    - Application opens it with open("/dev/can...", O_RDWR)
 *    - Uses driver-specific ioctls / read/write of driver-defined structs
 *      (there is NO single “/dev/can0 + SocketCAN” standard like Linux)
 *    - You must consult:
 *        * Raspberry Pi 4 QNX BSP documentation
 *        * canctl / canmsg utilities if supplied with BSP
 *        * driver header (e.g., <can/can.h> or BSP-specific header)
 *      to discover the exact frame struct and API.
 *    - Then adapt that struct -> can_frame_t inside can_adapter_receive().
 *
 *  Situation B — No QNX CAN driver exists for the Pi carrier / HAT:
 *    - Keep using simulated_can_adapter.c
 *    - OR implement a UART/SPI bridge where a microcontroller handles CAN
 *      and QNX talks to it via /dev/spi* or /dev/ser*.
 *
 * Example skeleton (pseudo-code, DO NOT compile as-is without real driver):
 */

